  (2, 'Amelanchier alnifolia', 'Berries & Edible Fruits', NULL, 'A small, deciduous shrub that can grow from 3–26′ in height. Bark is reddish-gray, leaves are oval-shaped to circular, with sharp teeth on their upper edges. Flowers are white, with 5 separate petals and sepals. Berries are purple, small, 5–15 mm in diameter, and ripen in early summer.', 'In forest undergrowth and canyons across most of western Canada and in the western and north-central United States.', 'Berries eaten fresh, made into “Indian pudding”* or added to pemmican and baked goods or frozen for later use. In addition, historically the berries were mashed and made into patties and dried for later use.
* “Indian Pudding” is made from berries cooked in water and sugar and thickened with cornstarch or flour.', 'sarvisberry-map.png'),
  (3, 'Prunus virginia', 'Berries & Edible Fruits', NULL, 'A shrub growing 3–19′ in height that often forms wide thickets. Leaves are oval-shaped, 1–3″ in length, with serrated edges. Small, white flowers grow in clusters and produce berries 6–14 mm in diameter that vary from bright-red to black in color. Ripens from August through September.', 'In forest undergrowth and canyons across most of Western Canada and in the Western and North-central United States.', 'Fruit can be eaten fresh. Historically made into patties or pudding and added to pemmican. Today made into “Indian Pudding” or added to baked goods. Wood was used for various tools like arrows, tipi pins, and stakes.', 'chokecherry-map.png'),
  (4, 'Shepherdia canadensis', 'Berries & Edible Fruits', NULL, 'A deciduous shrub, 3–13′ in height. Leaves are 2½″ in length, narrow and oval-shaped; green in color but whitish-brown underneath. Flowers are brown, 2 to 4 mm long with yellow-green sepals. Berries are deep-red, oval, and 5–7 mm long.', 'Undergrowth of forests, woodlands, and plains.', 'Berries eaten fresh, made into “Indian pudding”* or added to pemmican and baked goods or frozen for later use. In addition, historically the berries were mashed and made into patties and dried for later use.
* “Indian Pudding” is made from berries cooked in water and sugar and thickened with cornstarch or flour.', 'buffalo_berry.png'),
  (5, 'Rubus idaeus', 'Berries & Edible Fruits', NULL, 'Biennial, 5–8′ in height, that bears flowers and fruit in its second year from side shoots of the main stem.  Leaves are oval-shaped; coarsely textured with light hairs and sharply defined toothy edges. Flowers grow in clusters of small white petals. Red fruit consists of several ‘drupelets,’ or nodes, around a central core. After picking, the core remains with the stem leaving a hollow center in the fruit.', 'Along streams in open forests and woodlands.', 'Berries eaten fresh.', 'raspberry-map.png'),
  (6, 'Sambucus racemosa var. melanocarpa', 'Berries & Edible Fruits', NULL, 'Shrub growing 7–13'' in height with soft stems and 5 leaflets, lance-like in shape and irregularly serrated on the edges. Flowers form in pyramid-shaped clusters at the end of branches. Buds are pink, then open to white or yellowish petals with a star-shaped axis of white stamens tipped in yellow. Fruit is purplish-black.', 'Along streams and moist open forests and mountain slopes.', 'Berries eaten fresh.', 'elderberry-map.png'),
  (7, 'Rubus parviflorus', 'Berries & Edible Fruits', NULL, 'A dense shrub up to 8′ in height. Leaves have 5 lobes up to 8″ in width that are palm-shaped, and fuzzy in texture. Flowers are 2″ in diameter, with 5 white petals and multiple brownish-yellow stamens. Fruit consists of several bright red, “drupelets,” or nodes, around a central core, and ripens in mid-to-late summer.', 'Moist to wet, open forests, thickets, and mountain slopes.', 'Fruit can be eaten fresh.', 'thimbleberry-map.png'),
  (8, 'Rhus trilobata', 'Berries & Edible Fruits', NULL, 'Shrub 3–6′ in height. Foliage consists of 3 oval leaflets with round lobes that emit a strong odor when crushed. Small yellow flowers produce slightly sticky reddish-orange berries which have an aroma similar to limes and a very sour taste.', 'Mountainous shrubland, pine, juniper, fir forests, and wetlands.', 'Berries eaten fresh, made into “Indian pudding”* or added to pemmican and baked goods or frozen for later use. In addition, historically the berries were mashed and made into patties and dried for later use.
* “Indian Pudding” is made from berries cooked in water and sugar and thickened with cornstarch or flour.', 'skunkbush_sumac-map.png'),
  (9, 'Ribes cereum', 'Berries & Edible Fruits', NULL, 'Shrub 3–6'' in height. Stems are fuzzy and slightly sticky. Leaves are gray-green, oval-shaped, 3–5 lobed with toothed edges. White to pink tubular-shaped flowers bloom in clusters of 3–5. Stamens are longer than petals. Fruit is a red berry ⅜" in diameter with long dried flower remnants.', 'Moist to dry, often stony soil of grasslands, prairie, woodlands, and open forest.', 'Berries eaten fresh, made into “Indian pudding”* or added to pemmican and baked goods or frozen for later use. In addition, historically the berries were mashed and made into patties and dried for later use.', 'wax_currant-map.png'),
  (10, 'Ribes aureum', 'Berries & Edible Fruits', NULL, 'Shrub 6–10′ in height. Stems are fuzzy and slightly sticky. Leaves are leathery in texture, green, oval shaped, with 3–5 smoothly edged lobes. Bright-yellow, tubular shaped flowers bloom in clusters. Stamens are red-tipped and the same length as petals. Fruit is an amber-red to black berry ⅜″ in diameter.', 'Woodlands, prairies, and forests near streambanks.', 'Berries eaten fresh, made into “Indian pudding”* or added to pemmican and baked goods or frozen for later use. In addition, historically the berries were mashed and made into patties and dried for later use.', 'golden_currant-map.png'),
  (11, 'Ribes viscosissimum', 'Berries & Edible Fruits', NULL, 'A shrub 3–6′ in height. Stems are fuzzy and slightly sticky. Leaves are 3″ in length; have a thick, rough surface; and consist of 3 rounded lobes with toothed tips. Drooping clusters of 4–15 white, green or pink bell-shaped sepals produce five whitish, green, or pink-tinged sepals and white petals. Stamens are as long as petals. Berries are black, ⅜″ long with a sticky, resinous coating.', 'Moist to wet forests, mountain slopes, often near streams.', 'Berries eaten fresh, made into “Indian pudding”* or added to pemmican and baked goods or frozen for later use. In addition, historically the berries were mashed and made into patties and dried for later use.', 'sticky_currant-map.png'),
  (12, 'Ribes oxyacanthoides', 'Berries & Edible Fruits', NULL, 'A shrub 2–6′ in height. Stems are covered with sharp bristles and have sharp, curved spikes at their joints. Leaves are heart-shaped with deeply scalloped edges. Inflorescence consists of 1–3 drooping flowers with whitish-green sepals and white petals. Stamens are as long as petals. Berries are ⅝″ wide and green, purple, or black in color.', 'Woodlands and mountain forests particularly near streams.', 'Berries eaten fresh, made into “Indian pudding”* or added to pemmican and baked goods or frozen for later use. In addition, historically the berries were mashed and made into patties and dried for later use.', 'gooseberry-map.png'),
  (13, 'Prunus americana', 'Berries & Edible Fruits', NULL, 'A large shrub, up to 15′ in height, with thorny branches. The oval-shaped leaves alternate on the branch; are 2–4″ long; dark green; and smooth and pale on their undersides. Small flowers with five, white petals and yellow tipped stamens can occur singly or in clusters. Spherical to oblong-shaped fruit is yellow to reddish-purple and 1″ in diameter.', 'Thickets, woodlands, mountain slopes of plains and valleys.', 'Berries eaten fresh, made into “Indian pudding”* or added to pemmican and baked goods or frozen for later use. In addition, historically the berries were mashed and made into patties and dried for later use.', 'plum-map.png'),
  (14, 'Vitis riparia', 'Berries & Edible Fruits', NULL, 'Woody vines that can climb or spread up to 82′. Bark is reddish and fissured, typically coming loose and falling off in peels. Leaves alternate on the stem and are 10″ long with coarsely toothed edges. Inflorescence is a 6″ loose cluster of flowers; small, fragrant, and white or greenish in color. Fruit is a ½″ diameter berry or grape, greenish-red to black in color.', 'Along exposed areas with good sun exposure and adequate soil moisture, such as riverbanks, forest clearings, fence lines and along road sides.', 'Berries eaten fresh.', 'grapes-map.png'),
  (15, 'Fragaria vesca', 'Berries & Edible Fruits', NULL, 'A stemless, 4–8″ tall plant that spreads indefinitely through a “runner” root system. Foliage in groups of 3, coarsely-toothed, green leaves. Flowers consists of 5 small, white petals with yellow centers and stamens. Berries are bright red, ½″ long, egg-shaped to conic, with tiny seeds raised on the surface.', 'Thickets, coniferous and riparian forests.', 'Fruit eaten fresh.', 'strawberry-map.png'),
  (16, 'Vaccinium membranaceum', 'Berries & Edible Fruits', NULL, 'An erect shrub growing up to 5′ in height. Leaves alternate, are 2″ in length; very thin and oval-shaped; with serrated edges tipped with tiny hairs. Flowers are urn-shaped to cylindrical, ¼″ long, and pale-pink to waxy-bronze in color. Fruit is bluish-purple to black, ½″ wide, and broader than tall. The huckleberry is the state fruit of Idaho.', 'Found at higher elevations in both pine and spruce dominated forests and in open meadows.', 'Berries eaten fresh.', 'huckleberry-map.png'),
  (17, 'Calochortus nuttalli', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, '6–18″in height with thin, linear leaves.Inflorescence consists of 1–3 flowers; 3 white petals and sepals tinged with lilac or magenta. A yellow base with a purple beard-shape radiates outward from the center.', 'Grasslands, sagebrush prairie, open forest, and woodlands.', 'Crows consider it to be poisonous.', 'sego_lily-map.png'),
  (18, 'Ranunculus glaberrimus', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A herbaceous flowering plant 1½–6″ in height with lower leaves that are thick, smooth, and oval-shaped. Flowers have 4–5 yellow petals. One of the earliest blooming wildflowers in Montana, sometimes as early as February.', 'Widespread throughout the state, but most common in western Montana. It co-occurs in habitats with juniper and sagebrush. Can be toxic to both livestock and humans.', NULL, 'sagebrush_buttercup-map.png'),
  (19, 'Oenothera biennis', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A biennial, flowering plant that grows up to 5′ in its second year. Leaves are lance-shaped and up to 7″ long and 2″ wide. Blooms are yellow, 2″ in diameter with four petals. Flowers open each day in the early evening, then close in the morning.', 'Present in Montana, but more common to the Eastern United States and Pacific Northwest. Found near streams, roadsides, and valleys.', NULL, NULL),
  (20, 'Solanum triflorum', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'low-growing, spreading annual herb in the nightshade family. Leaves and stems are covered with tiny fine hairs. Leaves are lance-like to oval in shape and cut into pointed, serrated lobes. Stems bear 2–3 white to greenish tinged flower clusters, each a centimeter wide when open. Berries are round, 9–14 mm thick, with a metallic green shade.', 'Prefers tilled, disturbed terrain for germinating. Grasslands, prairie-dog towns, streambanks, and roadsides.', NULL, 'cutleaf_nightshade-map.png'),
  (21, 'Solanum physalifolium', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A taprooted annual of the nightshade family that grows to a height of 4–35″. Leaves are egg-shaped to triangular, with edges that are toothed or wavy. Sticky, soft hairs cover stems and leaves. Flower clusters have white to pale-blue petals; emerge from the joints of stems and leaves; and are visible from May through October. Sepals form a cup around the small (about 6–7 mm), round, yellow to dark brown berries.', 'Fields, along roadsides, and in other previously tilled sites, preferring moist soil.', NULL, 'groundcherry_nightshade-map.png'),
  (22, 'Castilleja linariaefolia', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A perennial herb that grows up to 3′ in height. Linear-shaped leaves appear alternately on the stem; are between 1 an 3″ in length; and envelop torch-like spikes of showy, bright-red bracts that resemble paintbrushes. Adopted as Wyoming’s state flower in 1917.', 'Grasslands, sagebrush prairies, and coniferous woodlands.', 'Historically the red tubular flowers were eaten fresh, especially by children. The leaves and stems are poisonous.', 'indian_paintbrush-map.png'),
  (23, 'Oxytropis lagopus', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A member of the pea family. Leaflets range in number from 5–15, and are covered with fine, silky hairs. Inflorescence consists of 5–20 magenta to bluish-purple flowers with white sepals. Oval-shaped legumes, or fruit, are enclosed within, or protrude from, the sepals. Chronic ingestion by livestock can cause a reduced appetite, a loss of weight in adults, and a drop in reproduction. It is the most widespread poisonous plant problem in the Western United States.', 'Sparsely-vegetated, stony soil of grasslands or dry woodlands, often on limestone.', NULL, 'locoweed-map.png'),
  (24, 'Nicotiana multivalvis', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'An annual herb growing up to 6′ in maximum height. The lower leaf blades can be up to 6″ long and emerge from short stems. The upper leaves are smaller and attach directly to the main stalk. Inflorescence consists of a broad, trumpet-shaped, white bloom with green or purple tinged edges, that is encased in a calyx of ridged, green sepals.', 'Sunny, open slopes and along well-drained, sandy floodplains.', 'This is the Sacred Tobacco of the <i>Apsáalooke</i> and is raised by specific officers of the Sacred Tobacco Society. Historically it was found throughout Crow Country, but today does not exist outside of cultivation.', 'sacred_tobacco-map.png'),
  (25, 'Tragopogon dubius', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'An annual flower that grows 15–31″ in height. Leaves are grass-like blades, ½″ thick and up to 1′ in length. Flowers bloom atop the main stem and branching stems and produce many pale-yellow, ray petals; and much longer, thinner, green bracts. Flowers open on sunny mornings and close by noon. The closed flowers resemble a thin pod, up to 5″ long.', 'Native to Central Europe and Western Asia.  Introduced and widespread to the United States. Found in grasslands, sagebrush prairie, and woodlands.', NULL, 'western_salsify.png'),
  (26, 'Cirsium arvense', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A perennial that can grow up to 5′ in length and spreads quickly through a complex underground root structure. Leaves alternate on the stem, are oblong, deeply lobed, and have very spiny edges. Flower heads are arranged so the outermost stalks are longer than the inner. Flowers are an inch in diameter with up to 100 pink-purple florets.', 'Moist, usually disturbed, or previously tilled, soil of fields, meadows, thickets, roadsides, woodlands, and open forests. A native to Europe, Canada Thistle is considered one of the worst invasive plants of North America.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'canada_thistle-map.png'),
  (27, 'Cirsium scariosum', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A taproot biennial having either no visible stem, or an unbranched stem up to 3′ in height. Leaves form in a rosette shape; have a surface of tangled, downy hairs; and sharp-toothed spines on their edges. It produces several flower heads of purplish-white disc-shaped florets. Provides forage for animals, particularly elk.', 'Moist to wet meadows and grasslands.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'elkthistle.png'),
  (28, 'Sarcobatus vermiculatus', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A deciduous shrub up to 10′ in height with spiny branches and green succulent leaves. Male flowers consist of 2 to 4 stamens concealed by a cone-like spike. Female flowers are green with red tinged edges. Provides forage for grazing animals, but can be toxic to livestock, especially sheep.', 'Prefers highly saline soil of the badlands and plains.', 'A hard wood that is used to make various tools. It received its names from historically being used as the drill of a fire starter or to pack tobacco into the pipe bowl.', 'greasewood-map.png'),
  (29, 'Erythranthe guttata', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A bee pollinated annual or perennial growing up to 31″ in height. Leaves are oval-shaped to round, with coarsely toothed or lobed edges. Yellow flowers form in up to 5 short-stemmed clusters; protrude from a purple-green calyx; and consist of upper and lower “lips.” The lower lip has many reddish-brown spots.', 'Shallow water of streams or moist soil of wet cliffs, seeps, and springs.', NULL, 'monkeyflower-map.png'),
  (30, 'Heracleum lanatum', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A tall perennial that can reach up to 10'' in height. Stems are hollow and covered with hairs. Leaves are large, divided into 3 lobes, and up to 16" across. Small white petals and minute sepals emerge from multiple stemmed umbels creating an umbrella-like shape.', 'Moist soil of mountain slopes and thickets, often along streams in forests and woodlands.', NULL, 'cowparsnip-map.png'),
  (31, 'Arctium minus', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A large and bushy biennial up to 6′ in height. Leaves are up to 20″ in width, dark green, heart-shaped to oval, with wavy edges. Flowers produce from multiple stalks; are pink to purple in color; and have long, protruding bracts ending in a slight hook. When dried, the flower heads become “burs” that attach by their hooks to passing humans and animals, which allows for the dispersal of their seeds.', 'Any disturbed, previously tilled, or cultivated soil.', NULL, 'burdock-map.png'),
  (32, 'Convolvuslus arvensis', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A perennial climbing vine that spreads through an underground root system. Leaves are linear to arrow-shaped and alternate on the vine. 5 petals fuse to form a trumpet shaped-flower, white or pale pink in color with five slightly darker pink, radial stripes.', 'A highly invasive plant and native of Eurasia, it is found in cultivated fields, pastures, gardens, lawns, roadsides, railroad beds, and waste places.', NULL, 'morning_glory-map.png'),
  (33, 'Asclepias speciosa', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'An erect perennial growing up to 4′ in height. Leaves are 4–8″ long; lance-like to oval in shape; and covered in soft, woolly hairs. Inflorescence consists of umbels of 10 to 40 flowers, pale-pink to pinkish-purple. Central flower forms a star shape. Fruits are green pods 2–4″ long filled with seeds attached to silky hairs.', 'Grasslands, meadows, fields, roadsides, and marshes.', 'Has medicinal uses. Historically boys used wadded leaves to make bullets for pop guns.', 'milkweed-map.png'),
  (34, 'Physalis pumila', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, '6–18″ in height. Leaves are oval to lance-shaped. Inflorescence is a single, pale, yellow-green flower head emerging from a drooping stalk . Tufts of short hairs cover the calyx (cup-like base) and stalk. Fruit is similar to the tomatillo, and loosely enclosed in a papery husk.', 'Dry prairies, rocky open ground, and roadsides. Native to the central United States, Texas to Iowa.', NULL, NULL),
  (35, 'Eriogonum umbellatum', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A perennial from a branched root system that emerge as loose, clustered mats. Leaves are spoon-shaped with a smooth to woolly texture. Inflorescence consists of many umbels of small, bell-shaped, white, yellow, or red-orange flowers from stems that rise 6–12″ above the lower leaves.', 'Sagebrush prairies and mountain areas.', NULL, 'suphur_buckwheat-map.png'),
  (36, 'Dodecatheon pulchellum', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A perennial with single, leafless flower stems, growing to a height of 2–15″. Leaves form at the base; are 2–6″ long; and lance-like to spoon-shaped. Each plant has between 1–25 flowers clustered at the stem top. Petals are magenta to lavender with a large white spot and dark ring at the base. Tubes are yellow and purple-black near the tip.', 'Grasslands to wet meadows of both valleys and mountainous areas.', NULL, 'shootingstar-map.png'),
  (37, 'Valeriana dioica', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'Stems 4–28″ tall. Base leaves are spoon-shaped to elliptical; smoothly textured; and 1 to 4″ long. Stem leaves are smaller, with 1–3 pairs of lateral lobes and a larger terminal lobe. Inflorescence is an umbrella-shaped cluster of small, tubular-shaped flowers. Roots have a musky or cheesy smell.', 'Dry to moist forest, grasslands, and meadows.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'valerian-map.png'),
  (38, 'Lupinus argenteus', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A perennial plant with one or more stems that that usually grow up to 2′ tall. The stems are branching; green to purple; with short spurs and fine straight hairs. Leaves are a palm-shaped compound of 6–9 silvery-green leaflets. Inflorescence is long cluster (up to 12″) of bluish-purple flowers, with a white or yellow spot on the uppermost petal.', 'Grasslands, sagebrush prairie, open forest, and woodlands.', NULL, 'lupine-map.png'),
  (39, 'Leucocrinum montanum', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A small herbaceous plant up to 4″ in height. Leaves are stemless and grass-like; up to 8″ in length; curved inward; with slight white edges. Flowers bloom in early spring and, like the leaves, are stemless and consist of 4–8 white, narrow petals, that resemble a star, with 6 thin, yellow-tipped stamens.', 'Grasslands of both plains and valleys.', NULL, 'starlily-map.png'),
  (40, 'Clematis columbiana', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'Deciduous vines up to 6–10′ long. Leaves are made up of three, lobed leaflets. Flowers are lavender to blue-violet; nodding downward; and have long, narrow, widely-separated petals.', 'Forested slopes, thickets, or dry, brushy hillsides.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'clematis.png'),
  (41, 'Veratrum viride', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A herbaceous perennial plant reaching 2–6′ in height, with a solid green stem. The leaves are spirally arranged; 4–14″ long and 2–8″ wide; broadly lance-like to elliptical in shape; heavily ribbed and hairy on the underside; and end in a short point. Inflorescence is a long drooping cluster of yellowish-green sepals.', 'Moist forest openings, meadows, and mountain slopes.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'false_hellebore-map.png'),
  (42, 'Iris missouriensis', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'An erect herbaceous perennial, 8–15″ in height. Base leaves are linear, 1 mm wide. Flowering stems are unbranched. Inflorescence consists of three spreading or drooping sepals, light to dark blue in color, with a yellow mid-stripe and blue veins; and 3–4 smaller, upright blue petals.', 'Moist to wet meadows thickets, woodland margins; plains, valleys, and coniferous mountain areas.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'blue_flag_iris-map.png'),
  (43, 'Linum lewisii', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A slender perennial growing up to 24″ tall, with spirally arranged, narrow, lance-shaped leaves that are ½–1¼″ long. The flowers are pale blue, or lavender to white; often veined in darker blue; with five, spoon-shaped petals. Flowers open at sunrise and fall off by noon.', 'Found at all elevations in grasslands, sagebrush prairie, woodlands, and roadsides.', NULL, 'flax-map.png'),
  (44, 'Cannabis sativa', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A tap-rooted annual up to 36″ in height. Leaves are up to 3½″ long; palm-shaped; divide into 5–9 narrowly lance-shaped leaflets; and have sharply-toothed edges. Female flowers are greenish, mottled with purple; and enclosed by a glandular bract. They are arranged in clusters, and can produce hundreds of seeds.', 'Native to Eastern Asia. Predominantly cultivated, but can be found in fields and roadsides.', 'A common recreational drug, both cultivated and imported, which is now legal in Montana.', 'marijuana-map.png'),
  (45, 'Nicotiana quadrivalvis', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'An annual herb growing up to 6′ in maximum height. The lower leaf blades can be up to 6″ long and emerge from short stems. The upper leaves are smaller and attach directly to the main stalk. Inflorescence consists of a broad, trumpet-shaped, white bloom with green or purple tinged edges, that is encased in a calyx of ridged, green sepals.', 'Sunny, open slopes and along well-drained, sandy floodplains.', 'Used ceremonially and for pleasure.', 'tobacco-map.png'),
  (46, 'Yucca glauca', 'Flowering Plants', NULL, 'A perennial plant that grows in colonies of rosettes or circular clusters. Leaves are up to 24″ in length; long and narrow, with sharp edges. Inflorescence emerge from stalks up to 40″ in height; are bell-shaped; and consist of 6 drooping, broadly overlapping, tepals that are white to pale yellow-green in color.', 'Stony or sandy soil of grasslands or badlands.', 'Historically the leaves were used to make cordage and the root for soap and shampoo.', 'yucca-map.png'),
  (47, 'Typha latifolia', 'Flowering Plants Aquatic', NULL, 'A perennial wetland plant up to 10'' in height and growing in 2–3'' of water depth. Flowers appear in June as dense clustered spikes atop the main stem. The male flower appears as tassels above the female, then falls off in August. The seed heads gradually break up in spring to disperse their tiny seeds to the wind.', 'Grows in flooded fresh water areas where the water depth does not exceed 3''.', 'Historically the fluff was used as an absorbent, such as in diapers and feminine hygiene.', 'cattail-map.png'),
  (48, 'Sagittaria cuneata', 'Flowering Plants Aquatic', NULL, 'An aquatic plant growing from a white or blue-tinged tuber, up to 13" in height. Leaves are arrow-shaped with 2 smaller, pointed lobes opposite the tip. Inflorescence rises from a stem above the surface of the water with several whorls of 5 white petaled flowers. Males have yellow stamens in the center.', 'Grows in slow-moving and stagnant water bodies such as ponds and small streams.', NULL, 'arrowhead-map.png'),
  (49, 'Nymphaea odorata', 'Flowering Plants Aquatic', NULL, 'Aquatic perennials with floating leaves and floating, showy, fragrant flowers. Leaves are 7″ long; green in color; nearly sphere-shaped; with a waxy surface and smooth edges. Flowers have at least 17 white, showy petals; 4 green or reddened sepals; and yellow stamens.', 'Introduced from the Eastern United States and found in acidic to alkaline lakes and ponds in valleys.', NULL, 'water_lily-map.png'),
  (50, 'Acorus americanus', 'Flowering Plants Aquatic', NULL, 'A perennial wetland plant. Leaves are bright-green, erect, blade-shaped, and up to 40″ in height. Flowers are inconspicuous and arranged on a thick, lateral, elongated, 3″ long cone. Flowers produce berries embedded within the cone.', 'Found in shallow water of ponds and marshes at lower elevations.', 'Has medicinal uses. Historically was traded from tribes to the east.', 'sweetflag-map.png'),
  (51, 'Balsamorhiza sagittata', 'Flowering Plants Culinary', NULL, 'A taprooted perennial herb with long hairy stems 8 to 22″ in height. The lower silvery-green leaves are triangular and up to 20″ in length. Leaves higher up the stem are linear-to-oval in shape and much smaller. The leaves have fine-to-rough hairs, especially on the undersides. Plants bear 1 or more flower heads that have a yellow-orange disc floret and 8–25 bright ray florets, 1–2″ long. Plant’s total height can be up to 30″.', 'Grasslands, sagebrush prairie, and valleys.', 'Roots have medicinal and culinary uses.', 'arrowleaf_balsamroot-map.png'),
  (52, 'Capsella bursa-pastoris', 'Flowering Plants Culinary', NULL, 'A small annual in the mustard family. Stems are 4–20″ tall and grow from a rosette of leaves with symmetrical teeth on their edges. Unbranched flower heads form from pedicels (smaller stalks) with 4 very small, white petals and 6 stamens. These produce flat, two-chambered heart-shaped seed pods.', 'Gardens, fields, roadsides, and streambanks ranging from plains to valleys. Native to Europe, but naturalized and common in many parts of the world especially colder climates.', 'Dried seeds can be used as a flavoring, similar to black pepper.', 'shepherds_purse-map.png'),
  (53, 'Helianthus annuus', 'Flowering Plants Culinary', NULL, 'An annual plant with thick bristly-haired stems that typically grow to 10′ in height. Leaves are broad, alternate on the stem, and are coarsely edged. Those near the bottom are larger and heart-shaped. The flower head can be up to 5″ in diameter; and is composed of many spirally arranged five-petaled, dark-yellow to red, disc florets that eventually become seeds. Ray florets, or outer petals, are deep yellow, orange, or sometimes red.', 'Disturbed, or previously tilled or cultivated soil of grasslands, streambanks, and roadsides. Traditionally grown on the edges of gardens as the ‘fourth sister’ to beans, squash, and corn.', 'Seeds are eaten raw or roasted.', 'sunflower-map.png'),
  (54, 'Plantago major', 'Flowering Plants Culinary', NULL, 'A perennial plant native to Eurasia. Leaves are 6–12″ in diameter and 2–8″ in length; oval-shaped; with sharp points and smooth edges. Flowers grow from a 6″ spike and are small, greenish-brown, with purple stamens.', 'Found in any area that has had disturbed or previously tilled soil, such as fields and along roadsides.', 'Leaves and stalks are edible.', 'plantain-map.png'),
  (55, 'Claytonia lanceolata', 'Flowering Plants Culinary', NULL, 'A wildflower that grows up to 6″ in height with lance-like to oval-shaped leaves. Flowers are star-shaped, consisting of white or pink petals with veiny stripes and a yellow patch near the center.', 'Moist soil of mountainous climates where the snow never melts.', 'Roots historically eaten and cooked in the manner that potatoes are today.', 'springbeauty-map.png'),
  (56, 'Lomatium cous', 'Flowering Plants Culinary', NULL, 'A perennial from a taproot, 3–12″ tall with no visible main stem above ground. gray-green compound leaves divide 3 times into further, smaller leaflets. Small, dense, yellow flowers cluster from short stems of various lengths from a common center stalk.', 'Dry, open, rocky places at low elevations. Often found near sagebrush.', 'Historically the roots were mashed, made into patties and dried for later use. Today it is used as a thickener, like flour or cornstarch.', 'biscuitroot-map.png'),
  (57, 'Musineon divaricatum', 'Flowering Plants Culinary', NULL, 'A multi-branched perennial herb from a taproot. Stems are 6–12″ tall and purplish at their base. Leaves are alternately divided into narrow segments from slender stalks. Flower heads emerge in clusters from multiple, short stalks and consist of 5 small, yellow or white petals.', 'Stony soil of grasslands and sagebrush prairie.', 'Roots are edible cooked or raw.', 'parsley.png'),
  (58, 'Lewisia rediviva', 'Flowering Plants Culinary', NULL, 'A low-growing perennial from a thick taproot with a rosette of thick, fleshy, circular leaves. Inflorescence is leafless and bears a proportionally large flower, with 5–9 oval-shaped sepals and 10–19 deep pink to lavender petals. Blooms April through July.', 'Well-drained, sandy or gravelly soil in grasslands and valleys.', 'Roots are peeled and boiled. Not as popular with <i>Apsáalooke</i> as it is with Tribes in Western Montana.', 'biscuitroot-map.png'),
  (59, 'Camassia quamash', 'Flowering Plants Culinary', NULL, 'A perennial up to 30" in height from bulbs. Leaves very narrow and grass-like from 4–24" in length. Pale blue to deep blue star-shaped flowers with 6 sepals and 6 yellow-tipped stamens that radiate from the top of the stem.', 'Deep soil of moist to wet meadows and grasslands.', 'Very sweet bulbs that can be dried and crushed into a natural sugar. Historically used as a sugar before the arrival of Europeans. Can also be roasted or boiled.', 'camas-map.png'),
  (60, 'Rosa nutkana', 'Flowering Plants Culinary', NULL, 'A shrub 2–10'' in height. Foliage consists of 5–7 paired, light green, elliptical-shaped leaflets with toothed edges and sharp prickles at the base. Petals are deep pink with a slight magenta tinge. Fruits are relatively large (1–2 cm) purplish-red hips.', 'Shorelines, floodplains, streambanks, meadows, open forests and forest edges.', 'Rose hips are eaten fresh or dried for later use. They are also boiled to make a tea. Bark and fruit have medicinal uses.', 'nutka_rose-map.png'),
  (61, 'Perideridia gairdneri', 'Flowering Plants Culinary', NULL, 'A perennial herb which grows to around 2–3'' in height. Slender, erect stems grow from cylindrical tubers. Leaves near the base of the plant are divided into many narrow, subdivided lobes. Leaves higher on the plant are smaller and less divided. Inflorescence consists of stemmed umbels creating an umbrella-like shape of spherical clusters of small white flowers.', 'Grasslands, meadows, woodlands.', 'Roots eaten fresh or dried for later use.', 'yampa-map.png'),
  (62, 'Montia parvifolia', 'Flowering Plants Culinary', NULL, 'A perennial herb growing erect to about 16" tall from a matted, branching base. Oval leaves alternate in rosettes, have a fleshy texture, and are 2½" in length. Flower stems bear 1–12 flowers each with five pink or white petals up to ½" long.', 'Wet, shallow soil along streams and on rock outcrops.', 'Leaves are edible.', 'indian_lettuce-map.png'),
  (63, 'Lepidium latifolium', 'Flowering Plants Culinary', NULL, 'A perennial that grows from 12–39″ with numerous woody stems. Leaves are bright green, oval-shaped, with toothy edges. Flowers are clustered in branches and have 4 small, white petals with 4 greenish-white sepals.', 'Invasive species that frequents areas that are moist or periodically flooded (wetlands, fields, pastures, grasslands, rangelands, roadsides).', 'Dried seeds can be used as a pepper.', 'pepperleaf-map.png'),
  (64, 'Allium cernuum', 'Flowering Plants Culinary', NULL, 'A herbaceous perennial up to 16″ in height. An unsheathed, elongated, conical bulb produces several grass-like leaves 2–4 mm in width. Inflorescence is a drooping cluster of pink or white, bell-shaped flowers with yellow-tipped stamens.', 'Found in deciduous woodlands to open grasslands.', 'Bulbs are eaten fresh or added to soups and stews.', 'nodding_onion-map.png'),
  (65, 'Thlaspi arvense', 'Flowering Plants Culinary', NULL, 'Annual plant, growing up to 24" tall with upright branches. Leaves are arrow- to heart-shaped. Inflorescence is a clustered spike of small white flowers with 4 sepals and 4 longer petals. Flowers produce flat, winged seed pods that contain 2 brown-black seeds which resemble pennies.', 'Native to Eurasia. Found in croplands, fallow fields, areas along roadsides and railroads, gardens plots, weedy meadows, and waste areas.', 'Seeds used to season food. Has a garlic flavor.', 'pennycress-map.png'),
  (66, 'Polygonum bistortoides', 'Flowering Plants Culinary', NULL, 'A perennial 4–20″ in height. Leaves are 2-8½″ in length; narrow; lance-like in shape; leathery in texture; and form close to the base of the plant. Inflorescence is a dense cylinder of tiny white or pink flowers with protruding stamens.', 'Moist grasslands, meadows in foothills and mountainous areas.', 'Roots can be added to soups and stews.', 'bistort-map.png'),
  (67, 'Pediomelum esculentum', 'Flowering Plants Culinary', NULL, 'A perennial 12 to 16″ in height. Leaves are a palm-shaped, 5 leaf compound, on a stalk up to 6″ in length. Inflorescence is a short, cylindrical, densely hairy spike of ½″ pea-shaped flowers. Flowers are light blue to purple. The upper petal is round with darker veins.', 'Found in sunny, sandy soil; grasslands, pine woodlands.', 'Tuberous roots can be eaten fresh or dried for later use. Added to stews and soups or boiled and eaten like mashed potatoes.', 'scurfpea-map.png'),
  (68, 'Asparagus officinalis', 'Flowering Plants Culinary', NULL, 'A perennial plant growing 3–5″ tall, with stout stems and much-branched, feathery foliage. Leaves are needle-like, modified stems, ¼–1¼″ in length, and clustered in groups of four. The flowers are bell-shaped; greenish-white to yellowish; and emerge either singly, or in clusters of 2–3 at the junctions of the branchlets. The fruit is a small red berry ¼–½″ in diameter.', 'Disturbed, or previously tilled soil; moist grasslands, meadows, fields, roadsides.', 'The young shoots, or “spears,” are edible.', 'asparagus-map.png'),
  (69, 'Verbascum thapsus', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A biennial plant that can grow up to 6′ in height. Its small, yellow flowers are densely grouped on a tall, pole-like stem that grows from a large, rosette of hairy, light green leaves. This dense spike of flowers can occupy up to half the stem’s length. Flowering lasts up to 3 months, from early to late summer. Can be known as “great mullein,” “common mullein,” or “cowboy toilet paper” in the Western United States.', 'Native to Eurasia and brought to North America as a medicinal herb in the beginning of the 18th century. Found in stony soil of grasslands, meadows, and open forests.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'mullein-map.png'),
  (70, 'Artemisia frigida', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A strongly aromatic perennial with a woody base and stems that spread out in clumps up to 1′ in height. Leaves are oblong-shaped and covered with dense, short, grayish-white hairs giving them a silver or white appearance. The inflorescence consists of 30-70 spherical, yellow florets each about a ½ cm wide.', 'Dry habitats like grasslands, shrublands, and woodlands. It has a tendency to increase in areas that have been heavily grazed by livestock.', 'Used medicinally and ceremonially.', 'fringed_sage-map.png'),
  (71, 'Ligusticum canbyi', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A perennial taproot from the carrot/parsley family growing to heights between 10–30″. Leaves are divided between several lance-like leaflets, ascending in pairs from a single axis. Flowers cluster from stems of nearly equal length that radiate from a common center stalk to form a slightly curved surface (common to most plants of the <i>Ligusticum</i> family).', 'Grows in moist, mountainous habitats, such as wet meadows and along streams.', 'Has medicinal uses. It received its <i>Apsáalooke</i> name due to its being traded from tribes to the west.', 'lovage-map.png'),
  (72, 'Ratibida columnifera', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'Medium-green, hairy stems growing 1–3′ tall. Leaves are hairy, with lance-like leaflets paired into 5–11 lobes. Inflorescence consists of a thick column of tiny purple disc florets, arranged in bands from the base upwards. This is surrounded by 4–12 drooping, ray florets that are yellow, brownish-red, or brown with yellow borders.', 'Grasslands, streambanks, fields, roadsides; plains', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'prairie_coneflower-map.png'),
  (73, 'Monarda fistulosa', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A herbaceous, aromatic perennial up to 3′ in height, that occurs in large clumps, due to a creeping lateral root system. Leaves are 2–3″ long, lance-shaped, with toothed edges. Lavender, two-lipped, tubular flowers appear atop square stems. Each flowerhead rests upon a whorl of pinkish, leafy bracts giving the appearance of a mop or a ragged pompom.', 'Grows in rich soils in dry fields, thickets, and clearings, usually on limy soil. The plants generally flower from June to September.', 'Has medicinal uses and as a perfume', 'horsemint-map.png'),
  (74, 'Urtica dioica', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A perennial plant that grows from 3–7′ with a widely spreading rhizome root system. Leaves are 1–6″ in length; alternate on wiry green stems; and have strong serrated edges. Inflorescence consists of small, dense, greenish-brown, drooping flowers. Stems can bear hairs of which tips come off when touched causing a painful sting.', 'Disturbed, often organic soil of meadows, open forest, stream banks; plains, valleys. Associated with nutrient rich soils, especially nitrogen and phosphate. Native to Europe, but widely distributed through North America.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'nettle-map.png'),
  (75, 'Taraxacum officinale', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A herbaceous perennial that grows from a taproot and produces several green, hollow, leafless flower stems. Leaves are basal, have jagged-looking edges, and are wider at the apex than the center. Many thin yellow ray florets project outward from the cup-like bract. As the plant matures, it creates feathery stalks containing seeds that are dispersed with the wind.', 'Originally introduced from Eurasia as a food crop and is now considered a weed. Thrives in moist soil of meadows, thickets, woodlands, lawns, and roadsides.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'dandelion-map.png'),
  (76, 'Actaea rubra', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A herbaceous flowering plant 15–30" in height. Leaves are egg-shaped with coarsely toothed edges. Alternately branched stems bear clusters of small spoon-shaped white petals. Flowers produce small green ellipsoid-shaped berries that eventually turn white or red in mid-to-late summer.', 'Shady areas with moist to wet soils.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'baneberry-map.png'),
  (77, 'Galium boreale', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A perennial flowering plant that grows between 8–20" in height. Dies back to the ground each winter, but spreads through a lateral root system. Narrow lance-like leaves attach directly to the stem in groups of four. Small, four-petaled, white flowers emerge in branched clusters from the stem.', 'Found in wet meadows, fields and woodlands in areas with calcium-rich bedrock.', 'Historically the roots were used to make dyes and paints.', 'bedstraw-map.png'),
  (78, 'Artemisia cana', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A perennial aromatic shrub growing up to 3′ in height. Stems and leaves covered in short, woolly, grayish-white hairs, giving it a white appearance. Lance-like leaves are 2–5 cm in length. Inflorescence consists of multi-branched clusters of spike-like flower heads with bracts forming cups around tiny, yellow disc flowers.', 'Sagebrush praires; plains, valleys, and coniferous mountain areas.', 'Used medicinally and ceremonially.', 'silver_sage-map.png'),
  (79, 'Matricaria discoidea', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'Stems are erect and branched, up to 16″ in height. Leaves are lance-shaped, and divided into pairs on opposite sides of the stem. Flower heads are cone-shaped, and densely packed with yellowish-green corollas. No ray florets. Leaves and flowers exude a pineapple scent when crushed.', 'Disturbed, often compacted soil of roadsides, fields, gardens; plains, valleys.', 'Used medicinally and as a perfume.', 'chamomile-map.png'),
  (80, 'Grindelia squarrosa', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'An erect biennial to perennial, growing up to 40″ in height, with multiple branches. Leaves are oblong with amber-colored resin-tipped teeth. Multiple flower heads with bracts that consist of overlapping rows of leaves with tips that curl outward. 24–40 yellow ray florets.', 'Found in dry, open areas and disturbed roadsides and stream banks.', 'Used medicinally and ceremonially.', 'curlycup_gumweed-map.png'),
  (81, 'Thalictrum dasycarpum', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'Tall erect plant growing up to 80" tall. Purple leafstalks produce compound (3–5) leaflets with shallow lobes and smooth edges. Inflorescence is clustered with purple stamens and light-green sepals.', 'Wet meadows, thickets, ditch and stream banks of plains and valleys.', 'Used medicinally and as a perfume.', 'meadowrue-map.png'),
  (82, 'Anthoxanthum hirtum', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A cool season grass 16–36" in height. Leaves alternate and are 7–12" in length. Flowers branch in loose clusters, ¼" long, green-tinged with purple, elliptic- to egg-shaped with a pointed tip. Blooms from May into June. The name comes from the vanilla-like fragrance of its leaves.', 'Southern Canada to Northern Plains and Rocky Mountain states. Found in marshes, meadows, wet prairies, and forest edges.', 'Used medicinally and ceremonially.', 'sweetgrass-map.png'),
  (83, 'Arctostaphylos uva‑ursi', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A woody, groundcover shrub growing 2–12″ in height. Branching twigs are pale-green and velvety in texture. Leaves are shiny, thick, and stiff, 1½″ long and ½″ wide. Urn-shaped flowers are white to pink and bear red to pink fleshy berries, ¼–½″ in diameter.', 'Dry coniferous forests.', 'Dried leaves are used as a smoking mix and have medicinal uses.', 'kiinnikinnick-map.png'),
  (84, 'Ivesia gordonii', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A perennial plant which produces a clump of erect stems and tail-like leaves that can reach a height of 10". Leaves are a thick, rounded strip of small, green, overlapping leaflets. Flowers grow in hairy clusters of five yellow-green triangular sepals and five spoon-shaped yellow petals.', 'Native to the mountain ranges of the Western United States. Stony sparsely-vegetated soil of rock outcrops, stream beds, grasslands.', 'Used as a perfume.', 'ivesia-map.png'),
  (85, 'Osmorhiza longistylis', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'Oval leaflets with serrated or lobed edges that divide into 3, then further subdivide into fernlike segments. 8–16 tiny, white flowers form from compound umbels. Each flower has 5 white petals; 5 white-tipped stamens; and 2 white styles which exceed the length of the petals.', 'Forests with fertile soil, often in areas of loam and dappled sunlight.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'sweet_cicely-map.png'),
  (86, 'Gutierrezia sarothrae', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A perennial shrub that is typically 4–24″ in height. Stems branch freely from the woody base forming a rounded shape. The stems die back during dormancy, giving the plant its broom-like appearance. Dense clusters of 3–7 small, yellow ray florets; and 2–6 tiny disc florets form at the end of the stems. The plant reproduces from seeds, which are light, densely hairy, and wind-dispersed.', 'Pinyon-juniper woodlands, desert shrublands, and sagebrush-grasslands.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'broom_snakeweed-map.png'),
  (87, 'Artemisia tridentata', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A many-branched evergreen shrub up to 10′ in height. Leaves are lance-like in shape; 1¼″ in length; and grayish-green in color; with fine, silvery hairs and a strong, pungent fragrance. Flowers are small, yellow, and form in tubular-shaped clusters.', 'The deep taproot and lateral root system allow it to gather water from many sources. Found in deep soils of prairies, steep open mountain slopes.', 'Used medicinally and ceremonially.', 'big_sagebrush-map.png'),
  (88, 'Artemisia dracunculus', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A perennial herb with slender branches up to 5′ in height that spreads rapidly through a lateral root system. Leaves are slender; lance-like in shape; 1–3″ in length; glossy-green in color; and attach directly to the main stalk. Flowers are ⅛″ in diameter, yellowish-green, and petal-less.', 'Open sites on dry sandy soils; sagebrush prairies, rocky slopes, and waste areas.', 'Used medicinally and ceremonially.', 'tarragon-map.png'),
  (89, 'Hedeoma hispida', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'An annual with erect stems, covered with short, stiff hairs, and up to 8″ in height. Leaves grow directly from the main stem; are ¾″ in length; and narrow with pointed tips and light hairs. Inflorescence is in groups of 2–12 blue to purple tubular-shaped flowers, ¼″ in length. Calyx is green, distinctly ribbed, and hairy with sharply pointed teeth. Calyx dries to light-brown, producing a 1-seeded nutlet.', 'Dry sandy or rocky soil of prairies, or along roads, railroads, and waste areas.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'pennyroyal-map.png'),
  (90, 'Achillea millefolium var. lanulosa', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'An erect, herbaceous, perennial plant that produces one to several stems 8–40″ in height. Leaves appear spirally and evenly along the stem, and are 2–8″ in length. The name “prairie dog tail” is due to the leaf’s fernlike appearance. Inflorescence is a flat-topped cluster of 10–40 white to pink disc florets.', 'Frequently found in the mildly disturbed soil of grasslands and open forests.', 'Leaves and flowers used to treat cuts and burns.', 'yarrow-map.png'),
  (91, 'Madia glomerata', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'An annual growing from 4–20″ in height, often in thick extensive colonies. Foliage is hairy in texture; studded with yellow resin glands; and strongly aromatic with an unpleasant scent. Inflorescence is generally a cluster of resinous flower heads with black-tipped yellow disc florets; and one or more tiny, greenish- or purplish-yellow, ray florets.', 'Moist, disturbed soil in open woods or grasslands; near shallow ponds or streams.', 'Used medicinally and ceremonially.', 'mountain_tarweed-map.png'),
  (92, 'Lithospermum ruderale', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A perennial with ascending-to-erect branched stems; 5–20″ in height; and covered with fine, upright hairs. Leaves are linear- to spoon-shaped; up to 4″ in length; and grow directly from the main stem. Bunches of greenish-yellow flowers with leaf-like bracts protrude amongst the leaves.', 'Many types of habitat; grasslands to open forests.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'stoneseed-map.png'),
  (93, 'Ceanothus velutinus', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A shrub growing up to 13′ in height and capable of forming colonies of dense thickets. Leaves alternate on the branch; are shiny-green; oval-shaped; and have minute teeth on their edges. Inflorescences are long clusters of white flowers.', 'Open forest, especially in recently burned areas.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'elkweed-map.png'),
  (94, 'Echinacea angustifolia', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A perennial 6–20″ in height. Stems and leaves are moderately hairy, erect, and mostly unbranched. Leaves are at the base of the plant; lance-like to spoon-shaped; and up to 6″ in length. Flower heads contains 8–21 pink or light-purple ray florets plus 80–250 orange disc florets.', 'Usually sandy or stony soil of grasslands and pine woodlands.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'coneflower-map.png'),
  (95, 'Osmorhiza berteroi', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A perennial herb with a branching stem up to 3′ in height. Leaves divide into 3 leaflets, each 3″ long, with toothed to lobed-edges. Inflorescence is a compound of 4–10 tiny white florets, radiating out from the main stem.', 'Forest, thickets, woodlands; from valleys to lower mountainous areas.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'mountain_sweetcicely.png'),
  (96, 'Artemisia ludoviciana', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A perennial growing up to 36″ in height. Leaves are stemless; 3″ in length; lance-like to oblong in shape; and covered in wooly, gray hairs. Inflorescences consist of multiple stems of drooping flower heads with green, overlapping bracts surrounding a tiny, yellowish disc floret.', 'Dry slopes, canyons, open pine woods, and dry prairies.', 'Used medicinally and ceremonially.', 'white_sage-map.png'),
  (97, 'Lomatium macrocarpum', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A spreading-to-erect perennial flowering plant from taproot, growing about 1½′ in height. The grayish-green base leaves divide into many small, narrow, feathery segments. Inflorescence is a compound cluster of white, to purplish-white, to yellowish flowers that emerge from lateral stems.', 'Grasslands or shallow soil of outcrops and open slopes of plains, valleys, or coniferous mountain areas.', 'Used medicinally and ceremonially.', 'biscuitroot-map.png'),
  (98, 'Helenium autumnale', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A perennial herb up to 4½'' tall. Leaves are dark-green, alternate on the branch, and lance-shaped. Each flower head has 11–21 yellow, short, wedge-shaped, drooping ray florets with as many as 800 yellow disc florets forming a spherical center.', 'Moist, often gravelly soil along streams, wetlands, and roadsides of plains or valleys', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'sneezeweed-map.png'),
  (99, 'Mentha arvensis', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A herbaceous perennial growing 4–24″ in height, with sprawling, squarish, purplish-green stems. Leaves are ¾–2½″ in length; opposite on the stem; and long and hairy with coarsely-serrated edges. Flowers grow in pale-purple whorls at the base joints of the leaf stems.', 'Wet meadows, fens, stream banks, wetlands of plains, valleys, or coniferous mountain environments.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'mint-map.png'),
  (100, 'Lomatium dissectum', 'Flowering Plants Herbal/Medicinal', NULL, 'A perennial flowering herb from the carrot family that can grow over 3′ in height. The leaves are small and narrow; typically smooth; and divide several times around long, slender stalks which attach to the main stem near the base of the plant. Its flowers are small red, purple, or yellow clusters on a leafless stem.', 'Sloped, rocky areas at the base of cliffs. May grow in bedrock or loose rock, and stony soils.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'fernleafbiscuitroot-map.png'),
  (101, 'Apiosporina morbosa', 'Fungi, Moss, & Lichen', NULL, 'A fungal pathogen that affects the branches of cherry, plum, apricot, and chokecherry trees. The olive-green “knots” can vary in size and be up to 12″ in length and 2″ in width. They become visible in late spring, and then spread and can kill the affected parts of a tree by autumn.', NULL, NULL, 'chokecherry-map.png'),
  (102, 'Evernia vulpina', 'Fungi, Moss, & Lichen', NULL, 'A bright, yellow-green, densely branched, coral-like fungi that grows on the bark of living and dead conifers.', 'Found in Douglas fir, Ponderosa pine, and lodgepole pine forests.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'wolf_lupine-map.png'),
  (103, 'Pleurotus pulmonarius', 'Fungi, Moss, & Lichen', NULL, 'The cap is convex-to-flat and up to 7–8″ in diameter. Gills are white and extend down the stem. The stipe, or stem, is very short if present at all.', 'Temperate and subtropical forests. In the west it is commonly found on or near conifers.', 'Has culinary uses.', 'oyster_mushroom-map.png'),
  (104, 'Sphagnum sp.', 'Fungi, Moss, & Lichen', NULL, 'Consists of a main stem, with tightly arranged clusters of branch fascicles (small, tight bundles), consisting of 2–3 spreading branches and 2–4 hanging branches. The top of the plant has compact green to yellowish-brown clusters that give the plant its characteristic tuft-like appearance.', 'Peat bogs, conifer forests, and moist tundra areas.', NULL, 'cattail-map.png'),
  (105, 'Bryoria fremontii', 'Fungi, Moss, & Lichen', NULL, 'A dark-brown hair lichen that grows as slender, hair-like, hanging strands from conifer trees or from small bushes. Main branches grow to be quite thick and usually become somewhat flattened, twisted, and wrinkled in older specimens.', 'Common in the mountainous areas of Western North America.', NULL, 'horsehair_lichen-map.png'),
  (106, 'Lycoperdon perlatum', 'Fungi, Moss, & Lichen', NULL, 'A medium-sized puffball fungus with a round body that tapers to a wide stalk, ½–2¼″ wide by 1¼–4″ tall. Its body is covered with spiny bumps or “jewels”. When mature, spores are released through the top in a burst when it is disturbed.', 'In woods under hardwoods or conifers, but also common along roadsides and in urban settings; late spring through fall in temperate regions.', NULL, 'puffball-map.png'),
  (107, 'Morchella esculenta', 'Fungi, Moss, & Lichen', NULL, 'The cap is yellow, to pale-brown, to grayish-brown, with rounded and irregularly arranged pits. Typically 1–3" wide and 1–4" tall. The stem is white to pale–yellow, hollow and straight, with a bulbous base.', 'The fungus fruits under hardwoods and conifers during a short period in the spring, depending on the weather, and is also associated with old orchards, woods, and disturbed grounds.', 'Used as a perfume or deodorant.', 'morel-map.png'),
  (108, 'Agaricus sp.', 'Fungi, Moss, & Lichen', NULL, 'Commonly known as the “cultivated button mushroom.” Cap is white, up to 4" in diameter, and may have fine scales. Young mushroom caps are hemispherical, but flatten out as the mushroom matures. Their gills are pink, but change to dark-brown over time. The stalk is between 1–4" tall.', 'Fields and grassy areas following rain, from late spring to autumn.', NULL, NULL),
  (109, 'Equisetum variegatum', 'Grasses & Reeds', NULL, 'A vascular plant with dark, blue-green, slender stems that are 30" in height and rough to the touch. The stem nodes are covered with a sheath that is marked with a black band and has dark teeth with white edges. Stems are capped with a green cone and black pointed tip.', 'Widespread throughout Montana, but most common in western part of the state. Wet, lime rich, soil along lakes and streams.', 'Whistles are made from the hollow reeds. Has medicinal uses.', 'horsetail-map.png'),
  (110, 'Agropyron smithii', 'Grasses & Reeds', NULL, 'A perennial grass growing up to 30" in height. Blades are stiff and curve inward. Sheaths and collars are hairless. Flowers are spiked, usually one per node, with 4–9 florets.', 'Open dry settings. Prairies, meadows.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'wheatgrass-map.png'),
  (111, 'Elymus canadensis', 'Grasses & Reeds', NULL, 'A perennial bunchgrass, growing as clumps rather than from sod, that reach heights of 3–5′. Leaf blades are flat and rough, ½″ wide and 8–12″ tall. Inflorescence is a nodding spike up to 10″ long with 5–20 spikelets.', 'Most common along road cuts where it has been likely introduced for soil stabilization.', NULL, 'ryegrass-map.png'),
  (112, 'Bouteloua dactyloides', 'Grasses & Reeds', NULL, 'A perennial, creeping, sodgrass that spreads through a “runner” root system. Stems up to 4" tall. Leaf blades are curly sheaths with overlapping edges, and gray-green, hairs on both sides. Inflorescence are spikelets (round globes topped with short spikes). Known as a “warm season grass,” it becomes green late in the spring and dries out early in the fall.', 'Disturbance-prone open dry sites including pastures, roadsides, and lawns', 'Used medicinally. Historically used to make a target for bow and arrow practice.', 'buffalograss-map.png'),
  (113, 'Zea mays L.', 'Grasses & Reeds', NULL, 'A tall, single stem grass ranging in height from 4–13''. The leafy stalk of the plant produces male tassels that produce pollen. Once pollinated, female ears, or corncobs, appear and are protected by leafy husks. Corncobs are filled with rows of usually yellow or white kernels. Some varieties produce orange, red, brown, blue, purple, or black kernels.', NULL, 'Used as a food.', NULL),
  (114, 'Toxicodendron rydbergii', 'Shrubs', NULL, 'Western poison ivy does not grow as a vine like <i>Toxicodendron radicans</i> (Eastern poison ivy). Instead, it is a shrub that grows up to and above 3" in height. Stems are long with three, shiny-green, egg-shaped leaflets that taper to the point. Leaves turn yellow to orange in autumn. Flowers are white with dark veins. Berries are small, round and yellowish. All parts contain urushiol oils that can cause severe skin rashes upon contact.', 'Common across all Montana and can be found growing in forests, and other wooded areas, usually near streams and rivers.', 'Has medicinal uses.', 'poison_ivy-map.png'),
  (115, 'Crataegus succulenta', 'Shrubs', NULL, 'Shrubs or small trees 15–50′ in height, with purplish-brown to gray branches, and sharply tipped ½–1″ thorns. Oval-shaped, serrate-edged leaves 1½–2″ in length. Flowers have five, small white petals that produce downy-covered red berries.', 'River bank thickets and woodlands; both plains and valleys.', NULL, 'hawthorn-map.png'),
  (116, 'Juniperus horizontalis', 'Shrubs', NULL, 'A low-growing, coniferous shrub with long, slender overlapping branches that hug the ground. Grows 4"–11" in height, but can be several yards wide. Male flowers are oval with yellow-brown scales that hold the pollen. Female flowers appear as white to reddish buds. The seed cone is berrylike, irregularly round, and bluish to blue-black in color.', 'Creeping juniper is intolerant of both shade and fire, so prefers open, dry sites like rock outcrops, sand dunes and sand barrens.', 'Used medicinally and ceremonially.', 'creeping_juniper-map.png'),
  (117, 'Juniperus scopulorum', 'Shrubs', NULL, 'The most widespread of North American junipers. A small, erect shrub or tree up to 20'' in height with ascending, flattened branches, and a conical crown. Bark is reddish-brown with a stringy texture. Leaves are dark–green with a waxy, blue-white coating. Cone seeds are berry-like, irregular in shape, and pale–blue to blue-black in color.', 'Found in the lower mountains and foothills where grasslands or scrublands transition to low forests.', 'Used medicinally and ceremonially.', 'rocky_mountain_juniper-map.png'),
  (118, 'Cornus sericea', 'Shrubs', NULL, 'A medium to tall flowering shrub, growing 10–13′ tall and 10–16′ wide. It spreads readily through an underground root system to form dense thickets. Branches and twigs are deep-red in color. Leaves are opposing; 5″ in length; egg-shaped to oblong; dark-green above and powdery-gray beneath. Flowers are dull-white and form a flat-topped, clustered compound. Fruit is a white berry .35″ in diameter.', 'Moist forest and thickets, especially along streams.', 'Used medicinally and as a smoking mix.', 'red-osier_dogwood_map.png'),
  (119, 'Symphoricarpos albus', 'Shrubs', NULL, 'A deciduous shrub that can spread and colonize into a dense thicket of stiff, branching stems. Leaves are oval–shaped, up to 2″ in length, and opposing on the stem. Inflorescence is a cluster of up to 16 bell-shaped, bright-pink flowers. Fruit is a fleshy, white berry.', 'Found in shady and moist mountain and forest habitats, in woodlands and on floodplains and riverbanks.', 'Used medicinally. Historically used to make brooms.', 'snowberry-map.png'),
  (120, 'Salix bebbiana', 'Shrubs', NULL, 'A large, fast-growing, multiple-stemmed shrub or small tree capable of forming dense, colonial thickets. 5–20′ in height with a short, twisted trunk up to about 8″ in diameter. Purplish twigs produce leaves 5″ in length; lance-like to oblong in shape; green in color; sparsely hairy above, while smooth on the underside. Small, dangling catkins yield spherical seeds covered with long, threadlike fibers that are dispersed by the wind.', 'Moist mineral rich soil along streams, wetlands, and valleys.', 'Wood used to make various tools.', 'diamond_willow-map.png'),
  (121, 'Alnus incana sp. tenuifolia', 'Shrubs', NULL, 'A broadleaf, thicket forming, multi-stemmed shrub or small tree, growing up to 20–40′ in height. The bark is thin, smooth, and ranges from green-gray, to grayish-brown, to reddish-brown in color. Leaves are very thin; elliptical- to oval-shaped; 3″ in length; and dull green on both sides, with doubly serrated edges. Male and female catkins appear in the spring.', 'Streambanks, lakeshores, or edges of bogs', 'Used as firewood.', 'thinleaf_alder.png'),
  (122, 'Lophophora williamsii', 'Succulents & Cacti', NULL, 'A slow-growing, low to the ground, cactus. It appears in groups flattened and ribbed spheres, that are blue-green or sometimes reddish-green in color; with areoles that produce soft yellowish, or whitish, woolly hairs. White or pink flowers bloom sporadically and produce a small, edible, pink fruit.', 'Primarily found in desert scrub and limestone-rich areas of Northern Mexico and South Texas.', 'Has ceremonial, spiritual, and medicinal uses especially by members of the Native American Church.', NULL),
  (123, 'Opuntia polycantha', 'Succulents & Cacti', NULL, 'A succulent up to 12″ in height that grows close to the ground and is capable of forming colonies several feet across. Its flattened and jointed pads are 7–10″ in diameter; oval or circular in shape; and bluish-green in color. The areoles are tipped with brown fibers and have spines of varying width. Yellow, red, or magenta flowers grow from the pear-shaped stem segments. The fruit is cylindrical, brown, and spiny.', 'Found in sandy soils of sagebrush prairie, Ponderosa pine forests, and pinyon-juniper woodlands.', 'Leaves peeled and juice applied to painted objects to stabilize the finish. Similar to a shellac.', 'prickly_pear-map.png'),
  (124, 'Escobaria vivipara', 'Succulents & Cacti', NULL, 'A small cactus, oblong or spherical in shape, that usually grows in groups. Individuals are up to 6″ in diameter. Densely covered with star-shaped patterns of white spines, with the center spines being orange or brown. Flowers are ½–2″ in diameter and bright-pink to purple in color. Its green, ellipsoidal fruit is 1″ in length, ½″ in diameter, and often covered with spines at its tip.', 'Grasslands, prairies; plains, and valleys.', NULL, 'pincusion-map.png'),
  (125, 'Pinus contorta ssp. latifolia', 'Trees (Coniferous)', NULL, 'A small, slender tree with whorled branches forming a conical crown that can grow up to 130''–160''. Bark is thin, scaly, and brown. Leaves are 4–8 cm in length, 2 per facile. Seed cones are oval-shaped but asymmetrical and 2-6 cm long.', 'From the upper, dry montane forests to the subalpine region of Western North America. Found on forested mountain slopes in the Western two-thirds of Montana.', 'Primarily used for tipi poles, but also for corral rails. Old poles, used for whatever purpose, should never be burned.', 'lodgepolepine-map.png'),
  (126, 'Pinus flexilis', 'Trees (Coniferous)', NULL, 'A high elevation pine growing 15–35'', but under favorable conditions can reach up to 65–75''. Needles are just over 3" in length and a dark, bluish-green. Bark is heavily creased and dark-gray. The name “limber” is due to its flexible or pliant branches.', 'Found through the Rocky Mountains, from Southwest Alberta to New Mexico.', NULL, 'limber_pine-map.png'),
  (127, 'Pinus ponderosa', 'Trees (Coniferous)', NULL, 'A large, coniferous pine up to to 200'' tall with an open, rounded crown and spreading branches. Thick, furrowed bark, with scales that resemble pieces of a jigsaw puzzle. Leaves are 3–10" long. Seed cones egg-shaped.', 'Found in mountainous areas from British Columbia south through Texas.', 'Historically pitch was chewed as a gum. Small balls, around marble sized, were made from the pitch to glue things together, especially the thick strands of men’s false hair.', 'ponderosa_pine-map.png'),
  (128, 'Pinus monticola', 'Trees (Coniferous)', NULL, 'Large trees up to 165'' in height. Crown is conical-shaped to round. Bark is gray with scaly patches and rusty-brown underneath. Branches slope upward and needles are slender and 4" in length. Cones are long and slender.', 'Found in moist forests and mountainous areas of Northwestern North America. It is the state tree of Idaho.', 'Historically pitch was chewed as a gum. Small balls, around marble sized, were made from the pitch to glue things together, especially the thick strands of men’s false hair.', 'western_white_pine-map.png'),
  (129, 'Tsuga heterophylla', 'Trees (Coniferous)', NULL, 'Medium-sized tree up to 200'' with a trunk up to 9'' in diameter. Bark is brown, thin, and furrowed. Leaves are yellow-green, thin and flat, 8–15 mm in length. Seed cones are small, narrow, and cylindrical, just over an inch in length', 'Most common in the Pacific Northwest, but can be found in mountainous areas further inland, where Western white pine is normally dominant.', 'Used medicinally and ceremonially.', 'western_hemlock-map.png'),
  (130, 'Picea pungens', 'Trees (Coniferous)', NULL, 'A spruce tree that can grow from 100–160'' in height. The bark is scaly and grayish-brown with a brownish-red undertone on its trunk. Needles are bluish-green, stiff, angled outward in fours, and sharply pointed. Seed cones are light brown, cylindrical, have flexible scales, and up to 4" in length.', 'Prefers cool and humid climates where most precipitation occurs in the summer. Found in moist mountain valleys of the Central and Southern Rocky Mountains, but most common in Colorado.', 'Used medicinally and ceremonially.', NULL),
  (131, 'Abies lasiocarpa', 'Trees (Coniferous)', NULL, 'A medium-sized evergreen conifer up to 66'' in height with a very narrow conical crown. Bark is gray with a brownish layer beneath. Leaves ⅝–1⅛" long, turned upward, with blunt-tips. The cones are erect, 2– 4" in length, dark-purple, then ripening to brown.', 'Occurs at high altitudes, commonly found at and immediately below the tree line. In Western Montana it can be found from 4,900–8,900''.', 'Used medicinally and ceremonially.', 'subalpine_fir-map.png'),
  (132, 'Thuja plicata', 'Trees (Coniferous)', NULL, 'Large evergreen trees up to 150–230'' tall with a conical crown, and reddish-brown, fibrous bark fissured into vertical bands. Branchlets are arranged in flat sprays. Leaves are glossy-green, and overlapping. Seed cones are ellipsoid-shaped, 8–12 mm long, with 4–6 pairs of woody, overlapping scales.', 'Found in areas with a wet climate along the Pacific coast. In Montana, it can be found in Glacier National Park.', 'Used medicinally and ceremonially.', 'western_red_cedar-map.png'),
  (133, 'Juniperus ashei', 'Trees (Coniferous)', NULL, 'Evergreen growing up to 33′ in height. Leaves are ¼″ in length and scale-like. The seed cones are round; soft; and berry-like; green, then maturing to purple.', 'Native from Northeastern Mexico to South Central United States. Considered an invasive species by ranchers.', 'Used medicinally and ceremonially. Traded from tribes of the Southern Plains. This is how it received its <i>Apsáalooke</i> name <i>Dúusbachee isbuluxpée</i>.', NULL),
  (134, 'Populus angustifolia', 'Trees (Deciduous)', NULL, 'A slender member of the willow family, up to 50–65′ tall, with upright branches forming a narrowly spreading crown. The bark is yellowish-green, smooth on its upper portions and furrowed on its older, lower portions.  Leaves are lance-like to oval-shaped, and alternate on the branch. Pendent-shaped catkins emerge in early spring. Oval-shaped fruit splits to produce seeds attached to long silky threads that can be dispersed the wind.', 'Near streams and creeks of coniferous forest at altitudes between 3,900–7,900''.', NULL, 'narrowleaf_cottonwood-map.png'),
  (135, 'Populus deltoides', 'Trees (Deciduous)', NULL, 'One of the largest North American hardwood trees growing from 65–100'' in height with spreading branches and a trunk up to 9'' in diameter. Bark is dark-gray and deeply fissured on mature trees. Leaves are triangular, 1½–4" in length with coarsely-toothed edges. Catkins appear in early spring. Males are reddish-purple in color and females green. Female catkins split open releasing seeds attached to cotton-like strands that can be dispersed by the wind.', 'Found near rivers and streams, especially those that experience frequent flooding.', 'Most popular for firewood, but also used to make tools and implements.', 'eastern_cottonwood-map.png'),
  (136, 'Acer glabrum', 'Trees (Deciduous)', NULL, 'A large shrub or small tree growing up to 20–30'' in height. Twigs are smoothly textured, and red to purple in color. Leaves are 5" wide, 3–5 lobed, with double-serrated edges. Inflorescence is a loose branching cluster of 5–10 yellowish-green flowers on short, leafy side shoots. Seeds are enclosed in “wings” that can be dispersed by the wind.', 'Moist, open, coniferous forests, riparian forests, and avalanche slopes; from both valleys to lower subalpine.', 'Wood used for making tools.', 'rockymountain_maple-map.png'),
  (137, 'Populus tremuloides', 'Trees (Deciduous)', NULL, 'Tree up to 82′ in height with spreading branches. Bark is smooth, pale, and becomes furrowed by black scars with age. Leaf blades are broadly oval to triangular; glossy-green above and gray underneath, turning to golden-yellow in autumn. Flower catkins form capsules of minute seeds that produce a cottony fluff for wind dispersal', 'Often found in large groves near streams, wetlands, and mountain slopes with moist soil.', 'Used for firewood and an indicator of water.', 'quaking_aspen-map.png'),
  (138, 'Ulmus americana', 'Trees (Deciduous)', NULL, 'Deciduous tree 69–115'' in height with gray to brown, fissured bark. Leaves 3–8" in length, elliptic- to oval-shaped with double-serrated edges, and rough to the touch. Flowers with red stamens appear from drooping stems. Fruit is a circular, yellowish, papery wing that encloses the seed.', 'Woodlands within ravines or along rivers and streams.', 'Wood used for making tools.', 'elm-map.png'),
  (139, 'Betula occidentalis', 'Trees (Deciduous)', NULL, 'A small tree or large shrub up to 46′ in height with a trunk 10″ wide. Bark is reddish-brown to black with slight peeling. Leaves are oval to square-shaped, 2¾″ long with sharply toothed edges and pointed tips. Female catkins are 1¼″ in length and composed of tiny, winged seeds that can be dispersed by the wind.', 'Found along streams in mountainous regions.', 'Used for making for various tools and sweat lodge structures.', 'water_birch-map.png'),
  (140, 'Salix amygdaloides', 'Trees (Deciduous)', NULL, 'A small tree or shrub 13–66′ in height. Twigs are yellow with a smooth texture. Leaves are 5″ long and 1½″ wide; lance-like in shape; finely-toothed on the edges; green above, and a powdery gray-green color beneath. Yellow catkins, 3¼″ long, emerge in branchlets among the leaves.', 'Found on prairies, often near streams, that co-occur with cottonwoods.', 'Used for making for various tools, especially drumsticks.', 'peachleaf_willow-map.png'),
  (141, 'Fraxinus pennsylvanica', 'Trees (Deciduous)', NULL, 'A medium-sized deciduous tree reaching 39–82'' in height. Bark is smooth and gray on young trees, becoming thick and fissured with age. The leaves are 6–12" long, oppositely A medium-sized deciduous tree reaching 39–82′ in height. Bark is smooth and gray on young trees, becoming thick and fissured with age. The leaves are 6–12″ long; oppositely arranged, with 7–9 leaflets; green above and underneath, turning to golden-yellow in the autumn. Flowers small and in clusters; male and female on separate trees; and are wind pollinated.', 'Found in alluvial soils along rivers and streams.', 'Used for making various tools, especially pipe stems due to the soft pith that can be easily removed.', 'green_ash2-map.png'),
  (142, 'Acer negundo', 'Trees (Deciduous)', NULL, 'A fast-growing tree that grows up to 35–80'' tall, with a trunk diameter of 12–20". Branches are smooth, somewhat brittle, and tend to retain a fresh-green color. Leaflets appear in clusters of 3–7, and are lance-like to oval-shaped, with slightly serrated edges. Flowers are drooping from the stem, small, and yellow-green in color. Seeds are wind dispersed by paired winglets.', 'Near ample waters sources. Forests, woodlands, roadsides, and fields.', 'Sap used to make “Indian Ice Cream.”', 'boxelder-map.png');






